Route notebook change-log reads through the backend

Fabric Spark cannot reach the CDC gateway, and a managed private endpoint
does not fix it. Private Link is only half a mechanism; the other half is
DNS, and Fabric integrates private DNS zones only for the resource types
it supports. Container Apps is not among them.

Measured from inside a Spark session, after the endpoint reported
Succeeded and Approved on both sides, both the app hostname and the
privatelink hostname still resolved to the public address. The connection
then reached the public Container Apps edge, which serves nothing for an
internal environment and drops the TLS handshake - an SSL error rather
than a refused connection, which is what made this look like a
certificate problem rather than a DNS one.

The backend already reaches the gateway over VNet integration, so
generated notebooks now read through it. The route mirrors the gateway's
own contract, so the notebook's parsing, its gap and corruption handling,
and its tests are unchanged; only the base URL differs.

The caller's bearer token is forwarded untouched. The gateway stays the
only authority on who may read a change log, and this route is therefore
not behind the Fabric token middleware that guards the rest of /api: a
Spark notebook holds no Fabric token for this application, and inventing
one would have meant a second, weaker authority.

The install guide previously recommended the private endpoint. That
advice was wrong and is now corrected in place, with the measurement that
disproves it, so the experiment is not repeated.

